#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055747} :activity/id #uuid "bfe70dfc-da4d-55b4-a070-178f1424a5d4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048470 :topic/id #uuid "a249df7c-110c-5944-a303-74505130a629" :topic/math-academy-id 28} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055970 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to evaluate $\\log_{4}9,$ but our calculator only has a key for the common logarithm $\\log$ (base $10$).\n\nIn such cases, we can use the **change of base formula**, which states that\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}.\n$$\n\nIt effectively allows us to change a logarithm from one base, $a,$ to another, $b.$ Note that $X,$ $a,$ and $b$ have to be all greater than zero and that $a \\ne 1,$ $b \\ne 1.$ \n\nWe usually choose the value of base $b$ that makes the problem easier. In particular, if we need to evaluate the expression using a calculator, we choose $b=10$ or $b=e$. \n\nFor example, to evaluate $\\log_{4}9$ using this formula, we can rewrite the logarithm from base $a=4$ to base $b=10.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡9 & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡9}{\\operatorname{log}⁡4} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0.954\\,242}{0.602\\,059} \\\\  & =1.585\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :tutorial/id #uuid "bb104b87-e850-5cff-9826-fdd61c0287d1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2797 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "59ad328c-2f78-5aea-b0d1-6ed4bc8f3800" :step/math-academy-id 467832 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055748}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055748 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054744 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054541 :question/id #uuid "15844e53-9573-5e6f-8fef-debbe0a142ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-4091"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87ee4eae-702f-59f0-aff4-46c9acb89f6c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048964}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 